// Find every AI workload in your Azure estate, and the one column that says
// anything about whether somebody approved it.
//
// WHERE TO RUN THIS: Azure portal, search "Resource Graph Explorer", paste, Run query.
// Deploy-C1M04AIWorkload.ps1 -Mode Show runs the same lookup from the command line.
//
// WHY RESOURCE GRAPH AND NOT DEFENDER FOR CLOUD. Defender for Cloud's Cloud Security
// Explorer and inventory read a security graph that publishes on a DAILY snapshot,
// so a workload you deployed this morning won't appear this afternoon. Resource Graph
// reads Azure Resource Manager directly and answers in about a second. When you're
// hunting for what exists RIGHT NOW, use this. When you want posture findings and an
// AI Bill of Materials, use Defender CSPM and accept the snapshot delay.
//
// THE POINT. Look at GovernanceState. It's a TAG. A human typed it. Nothing validates
// it, nothing requires it, and deleting it changes nothing about whether the workload
// keeps serving traffic. That's the difference between an inventory and governance,
// and it's what the module's adoption gate exists to fix.
resources
| where type =~ 'microsoft.cognitiveservices/accounts'
| where resourceGroup =~ 'rg-globomantics-c1m04'
| project name, kind, location, GovernanceState = tags['governance-state']

// Widen it to your whole estate by deleting the resourceGroup line. Add Azure Machine
// Learning workspaces by extending the type filter:
//
// | where type =~ 'microsoft.cognitiveservices/accounts'
//      or type =~ 'microsoft.machinelearningservices/workspaces'
//
// Make the tag mandatory with the built-in Azure Policy "Require a tag on resources"
// assigned at a management group with a Deny effect. That gets you a field that's
// always populated. It doesn't get you a field that's always true.
